1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,463
Give yourself two weeks to finish

2
00:00:02,463 --> 00:00:05,000
a project and it will mysteriously

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:06,418
take fourteen days.

4
00:00:06,418 --> 00:00:09,329
Give yourself two hours before catching

5
00:00:09,329 --> 00:00:10,001
a flight,

6
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:11,941
and you finish it with ten

7
00:00:11,941 --> 00:00:13,210
minutes to spare.

8
00:00:13,210 --> 00:00:15,332
In a 1955 essay in The Economist,

9
00:00:15,332 --> 00:00:18,355
historian Cyril Northcote Parkinson pointed out

10
00:00:18,355 --> 00:00:20,091
that between 1914 and 1928,

11
00:00:20,091 --> 00:00:22,342
the Royal Navy's capital ships fell

12
00:00:22,342 --> 00:00:23,629
by about two-thirds,

13
00:00:23,629 --> 00:00:27,230
while Admiralty officials grew by seventy-eight percent.

14
00:00:27,230 --> 00:00:29,616
Long deadlines rarely create masterpieces;

15
00:00:29,616 --> 00:00:31,093
they invite perfectionism,

16
00:00:31,093 --> 00:00:32,456
anxious second-guessing,

17
00:00:32,456 --> 00:00:33,820
and decorative busywork.

18
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:35,922
Tight constraints force your brain to

19
00:00:35,922 --> 00:00:37,910
execute only what moves the needle.

20
00:00:37,910 --> 00:00:40,078
That is why a shorter deadline

21
00:00:40,078 --> 00:00:40,945
often helps:

22
00:00:40,945 --> 00:00:43,401
shipping version one in three days

23
00:00:43,401 --> 00:00:45,569
instead of two weeks lets real

24
00:00:45,569 --> 00:00:47,520
feedback guide version two.

25
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:50,467
Take your most important task tomorrow,

26
00:00:50,467 --> 00:00:52,735
slice the time budget in half,

27
00:00:52,735 --> 00:00:54,699
and start a visible timer.

28
00:00:54,699 --> 00:00:57,420
Watch how fast the fluff disappears.
